Introduction

Within the ASML TubeDG multi-disciplinary team, we study and optimize the tin droplet generator's dynamic behavior in order to enable future increases in EUV power. To achieve this, we use vibrometer measurements to reconstruct a 3D harmonic response of key components. 

Your Assignment

In this internship, you will develop a method to align vibrometer measurements using computer vision and machine learning techniques. Vibrometer is an interferometer mounted on a (x,y,z) stage, enabling measurements of 2D harmonic oscillation responses. To extend the capabilities of the setup, a rotary stage has been added. By rotating and recording multiple 2D oscillation responses from different viewing angles, it becomes possible to reconstruct 3D harmonic oscillations of e.g. tubular machine components. However, because the tubular components are not perfectly symmetric and aligned, each rotated measurement requires manual annotation and alignment. This process is time-consuming, subjective and prone to error. The aim of this internship is to replace the manual alignment step with a robust computer vision-based alignment algorithm.


The main tasks include:

Conduct a literature study on relevant computer vision and machine learning methods and evaluate how effective they can be applied for measurement alignment;

Develop a robust computer vision based algorithm to automatically align vibrometer measurements;

Perform experimental tests on the vibrometer setup to validate the developed method;

Improve the accuracy and efficiency of the measurement workflow, including reducing the number of required measurements;

Document, report and present the methodology, results and conclusions.

This is a Bachelor or Master internship for 5-6 months, working 4-5 days per week (3 days onsite).The topic is also suited for a thesis. The start date of this internship is as soon as possible.

Your Profile

To be a fit for this assignment, you:

Are busy with your bachelor's or master's in Computer Science or Engineering;

Possess knowledge of computer vision and machine learning;

Have some programming experience in Python (OpenCV, NumPy, Tensorflow/Keras) and also MATLAB;

Have an understanding of or interest in vibration and dynamics;

Have good communication skills in English (verbal and written).



