Introduction

Within the ASML TubeDG multi-disciplinary team, we study and optimize the tin droplet generator's dynamic behavior in order to enable future increases in EUV power. A thorough understanding of the underlying physics, together with accurate models that represent the system’s behaviour, is essential for the success of our work.

Your Assignment

In this internship, you will experimentally characterize the interaction of liquid metal and structural resonances of components to get a better understanding of the involved mechanism and identify bandwidth limitations of different FEM modelling approaches. The main tasks include:

Familiarize yourself with the topic through literature study;

Design or develop a suitable experimental setup, using liquid Gallium instead of tin;

Develop a FEM model in Comsol to compare different modeling approaches

Analyse structural resonances of the test structure with laser vibrometry;

Validate the FEM model and make adjustments on the material parameters;

Measure the fluid actuation as a result of structural oscillation and compare it with FEM models;

Report and present findings.

This is a Bachelor or Master internship for 5-6 months, working 4-5 days per week (3 days onsite).The topic is also suited for a thesis. The start date of this internship is as soon as possible.

